# rsync-sbx allowlist.  Copy this file to `allowlist` in the same directory on
# the sandbox box (/opt/sbx/tools/rsync/allowlist, bound read-only into the job
# as /deskbox/tool/allowlist) and edit the entries.  The tool reads only
# `allowlist`; this .example file is a template and is never read.
#
#   <name> = [user@]<host>[:port]  /absolute/prefix
#
# The source or destination names a remote side as <name>:<absolute path>.  The
# name must match an entry below exactly, and the path must be the entry's
# prefix or below it (a missing, relative or "/" prefix makes the file invalid).
# The user and port come only from this file, never from the path the model writes.  No wildcards, no CIDR ranges,
# no default: an empty, missing or malformed file permits no remote host.
#
# A net tool's job gets /etc/resolv.conf bound in by sbx-run, so a hostname
# resolves; an IPv4 address also works.  SSH expects id_ed25519 and known_hosts
# in this same directory.
#
# backup = ryan@backup.example.com  /srv/backups
# nas    = 10.0.0.10:22  /srv/share
